title: Converse_Heim
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
<<if visited("Converse_Heim_Close")>>
    <<jump Gate_Metaphysics>>
<<endif>>
Heim: You reach out your hand to touch a glowing lantern suspended in the virtual forest.
Heim: Your eyes see its light and your fingers register its form through haptic gloves.
Heim: Yet the lantern is constructed entirely of binary code executing on microprocessors.
Heim: Does that digital lantern exist as a genuine, real object, or is it merely an elaborate optical illusion?
-> It exists as a real object made of digital structures.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Code>>
-> It is a fictional representation, like a mountain in a painting.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Fictional>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Code
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: That is the core commitment of virtual realism, a concept I put forward and Chalmers expanded.
Heim: Digital objects have genuine causal powers, location, and persistent structure in computer memory.
Heim: However, a virtual lantern cannot burn physical wood or illuminate a dark room in the physical world.
Heim: If its effects are restricted to software, how can we claim it is real in the same way physical matter is real?
-> I accept that virtual reality is a distinct kind of real existence.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Code_CONCEDE>>
-> If it cannot interact with physical matter, it is not truly real.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Code_PUSHBACK>>
-> How do digital objects exercise real causal powers?
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Code_ASK>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Code_CONCEDE
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: Your concession establishes a crucial metaphysical distinction.
Heim: Reality is not identical to physical material; digital objects constitute a real non-physical realm.
Heim: We expand our ontology to include digital entities alongside physical and mental ones.
Heim: Once we grant digital existence, virtual worlds gain genuine ontological standing.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Turn>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Code_PUSHBACK
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: You insist that physical interaction is the sole criterion for genuine reality.
Heim: Yet mathematical structures, economic currencies, and legal institutions exert real effects without physical weight.
Heim: Digital objects operate within their own domain, causing changes to data states and human behavior alike.
Heim: Restricting reality strictly to physical atoms ignores vast domains of real human experience.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Turn>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Code_ASK
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: They exercise power by altering server states, triggering display pixels, and guiding user decisions.
Heim: When a player moves a virtual object, data instructions execute deterministically across network hardware.
Heim: Cause and effect operate reliably within the software environment.
Heim: Causal interaction within a system is the hallmark of real existence.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Turn>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Fictional
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: You take the path of virtual fictionalism.
Heim: On this view, interacting with a virtual lantern is simply engaging in a game of make-believe.
Heim: Yet unlike a static painting or novel character, digital objects persist independently of your imagination and respond dynamically to inputs.
Heim: Can a interactive environment that alters server memory be reduced to mere fiction?
-> I concede that interactive data differs from static fiction.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Fictional_CONCEDE>>
-> Fictions can be interactive without becoming real entities.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Fictional_PUSHBACK>>
-> What separates a digital object from a fictional story?
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Fictional_ASK>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Fictional_CONCEDE
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: You recognize that interactivity and persistence transform the nature of the object.
Heim: When code maintains state changes on a server, it steps beyond human imagination into autonomous digital reality.
Heim: Fiction resides in human thought; software state resides in electronic hardware.
Heim: That shift marks the boundary between make-believe and virtual realism.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Turn>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Fictional_PUSHBACK
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: You maintain that interactivity is just a sophisticated narrative mechanism.
Heim: Yet when two people trade digital property in a virtual world, wealth and ownership change in the physical economy.
Heim: If virtual actions produce real legal and financial consequences, calling the objects fictional misses their tangible impact.
Heim: Real consequences demonstrate that we are dealing with more than stories.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Turn>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Fictional_ASK
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: The key distinction lies in mind-independence and causal feedback.
Heim: Sherlock Holmes does not exist when no one reads the book, nor does he react if you talk to him.
Heim: A virtual object exists as running code on a server and responds according to programmed laws of physics.
Heim: Dynamic software state gives virtual objects a reality that stories never achieve.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Turn>>
===

// FUTURE FIX: lines 2-6 of this node and Converse_Heim_Verdict_IDEALISM were redrafted by Claude on 2026-11-11
// to make the objection Berkeleyan (to be is to be perceived) instead of a server-hardware objection. Jim to revise.
title: Converse_Heim_Turn
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: Now consider the strongest objection raised against my virtual realism.
Heim: It is likely that we might think Berkeley and other idealists would likely argue that digital objects are just display signals dependent on human perception.
Heim: For Berkeley, to be is to be perceived, so a virtual lantern is as real as a tree, and the tree no more real than the lantern.
Heim: I respond that virtual objects possess persistent data structures independent of any single viewer, making them real digital items rather than subjective illusions.
Heim: Still, Berkeley would answer that the tree persists too, in the mind of God, so persistence alone may not separate the two.
-> Virtual realism successfully establishes that digital objects are real.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Verdict_REALISM>>
-> Berkeley is right that virtual and physical objects are equally ideas.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Verdict_IDEALISM>>
-> Virtual objects form a completely new ontological category of their own.
    <<jump Converse_Heim_Verdict_CATEGORY>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Verdict_REALISM
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: You embrace virtual realism.
Heim: Recognizing digital objects as real expands our metaphysics to match the world we are building.
Heim: It allows us to treat virtual experiences with serious philosophical weight.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Close>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Verdict_IDEALISM
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: You side with Berkeley, for whom virtual and physical objects are alike ideas in a mind.
Heim: Then virtual realism wins too cheaply, because nothing is real in the sense the materialist meant.
Heim: The question becomes whether my digital objects need anything more than being perceived.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Close>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Verdict_CATEGORY
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: You propose a third ontological option.
Heim: Neither pure physical matter nor pure mental fantasy, digital objects create a distinct domain of human reality.
Heim: That insight helps bridge traditional philosophy with digital technology.
<<jump Converse_Heim_Close>>
===

title: Converse_Heim_Close
tags: converse
area: Metaphysics
speaker: Heim
---
Heim: As virtual worlds grow richer, deciding what counts as real becomes an urgent task.
Heim: Consider whether virtual structures alter how you understand the physical world around you.
Heim: Carry these metaphysical distinctions with you as you walk deeper into the grove.
<<jump Gate_Metaphysics>>
===
